{-
   A condition variable - wait until somebody says the world has changed.

   The primitive for "this thread cannot proceed until a *predicate* becomes true", where the
   predicate is over state a mutex protects and is too complicated to be a word. A semaphore counts;
   a condition says nothing at all about what changed, which is why every wait is inside a loop that
   re-checks.

   § Why it needs the mutex

   The classic race, and the reason `wait` takes the lock rather than the caller releasing it first:

     1. the waiter, holding the lock, finds the predicate false;
     2. it releases the lock and prepares to wait;
     3. the notifier takes the lock, makes the predicate true, and notifies - and nobody is waiting;
     4. the waiter waits, on something that already happened.

   `wait` closes it by reading a **sequence number** before releasing the lock, and handing that
   number to the kernel as its expectation. A notifier that arrives in the window has already
   incremented the sequence, so the kernel refuses to park and the waiter goes round again. The
   number never means anything by itself; what it is for is to have changed.

   § Every wait is a loop, and that is not a caveat

       m.withLock()
       while !ready(shared):
           wait(c, m)

   A `wait` may return without any notification at all - a signal, a wake meant for another waiter, a
   sequence that moved for an unrelated reason - and there is deliberately no attempt to filter those
   out. A caller that re-checks its own predicate is correct under all of them; one that trusts a
   single return is wrong even under a perfect implementation, because a third thread may have taken
   the state back between the notify and the wake.

   § What is not here

   `notifyAll` wakes every waiter, and all but one of them will immediately contend for the mutex and
   go back to sleep - the thundering herd. Linux has `FUTEX_REQUEUE` for exactly this, which moves
   waiters from the condition's queue onto the mutex's without waking them. It is not here because it
   needs the mutex's own word and its three states to line up with what the kernel expects, and that
   is a correctness argument to make with measurements rather than one to guess at.

   There is no timed wait yet, and no `waitUntil` over a predicate.
-}
import Native
import Atomic
import Native.Thread.Linux

pub data Condition {sequence: Atomic(U32)}

pub fn condition() -> Condition = Condition {sequence: atomic(0 :: U32)}

{-
   `wait(self, held)` - release the mutex, wait, and take it again.

   The caller must hold `held`, and holds it again on return. Between those two the lock is *not*
   held, which is the whole point and the reason the predicate has to be re-read afterwards.

   The order of the three steps is the entire correctness argument. Reading the sequence **before**
   releasing the lock is what makes the expectation meaningful: any notifier that could have run
   since must have taken the lock, so it ran after this read and its increment is visible to the
   kernel's comparison. Releasing before parking is what lets a notifier run at all.
-}
pub fn wait(self: Condition, held: Mutex) -> {}:
    let observed = self.sequence.load(LoadAcquire)

    held.unlock()
    futexWait(self.sequence, observed)
    held.lock()

{-
   Start one waiter.

   The increment has to come first and has to be an atomic read-modify-write: it is what makes a
   parked waiter's expectation stale, and what makes a waiter that has *not* parked yet fail to. A
   store would lose a concurrent notifier's increment and leave a waiter parked on a number that
   never changes again.

   Release, because what the waiter is about to look at is whatever this thread changed under the
   lock. The lock's own release orders that too, and stating it here as well costs nothing on this
   architecture and one line of reasoning less at the call site.
-}
pub fn notifyOne(self: Condition) -> {}:
    self.sequence.fetchAdd(1, UpdateRelease)
    futexWake(self.sequence, 1)

-- Start every waiter. The right form when what changed can let more than one of them through, and
-- the honest form when the code does not know - a missed wakeup is a hang and a spurious one is a
-- loop iteration.
pub fn notifyAll(self: Condition) -> {}:
    self.sequence.fetchAdd(1, UpdateRelease)
    futexWake(self.sequence, 0x7FFFFFFF)
